+      "body": "## Summary\r\n\r\nFix #1752.\r\n\r\nThis PR implements a new CLI command `@biomejs/biome migrate eslint` to migrate from ESLint to Biome.\r\nThe command updates `biome.json` using the ESLint configuration file available in the working directory (unlike ESLint, we don't look for the config file in parent directories if the config file is not found in the working directory).\r\n\r\nNote that this is a **best-effort** approach. It is unlikely that you will get the same behavior as ESLint. Biome has chosen not to implement many of the options provided by ESLint.\r\n\r\nWe support both the legacy config format and the new flat config format in JSON, JS/CJS/MJS.\r\nJS, CJS, and MJS config files are first evaluated and serialized in JSON using `node --eval \"...\"`.\r\nThe resulting JSON is then deserialized by Biome.\r\n\r\nWe emit an unsupported error for legacy config in the YAML format.\r\n\r\nOnce deserialized, Biome resolves the `extends` field to obtain the \"final\" config file.\r\nWe support shared configs and plugin configs by invoking `node --eval`.\r\nWe also support ESLint's presets such as `eslint:recommended`.\r\n\r\nThen Biome converts the obtained ESLint config into its own config format.\r\nWe support `globals`, `rules`, `ignorePatterns`, and `overrides`.\r\n\r\nWe rely on Biome's rule metadata to determine the equivalent Biome rule for a given ESLint rule.\r\nI created a new codegen script named `migrate-eslint` to generate a mapping between ESLint's rules and Biome's rules.\r\nThis conversion step also includes a handwritten part that translate some ESLint rule options to the corresponding Biome.\r\n\r\nBy default, `nursery` and `inspired` rules are excluded from the migration.\r\nThe `migrate eslint` command has two flags to change this: `--include-nursery` and `--include-inspired`.\r\nIf, during a migration, there is at least one inspired rule that can be migrated, then the CLI suggests using `--include-inspired`.\r\n\r\nWe then merge the current biome config and the obtained biome config.\r\nThe obtained config overrides the current Biome config.\r\nThis means that if you have a Biome config file that disables a rule enabled in the ESLint config file, the rule will be enabled after you run `biome migrate eslint`.\r\n\r\nThe following config files are not supported:\r\n- Legacy configs in YAML;\r\n- Custom config names (the config file is passed with the `--config` CLI option to ESLint).\r\n\r\nTo evaluate `.js` and `.cjs` config files, Biome invokes `node`.\r\n\r\nAlso, `biome migrate eslint` migrates ESLint ignore file `.eslintignore`.\r\nIt emits a warning if it encounters a negated glob pattern because we don't support them.\r\nNote that Biome doesn't support `.eslintignore` in nested directories.\r\n\r\n## Test Plan\r\n\r\nI added several unit tests.\r\n\r\nNOTE: I didn't write unit test for ESLint config files in JS/CJS because this invokes `node`.\r\n",
